cartridge "HOME RUN DERBY/AUTHOR UNKNOWN (RERUN 1985)/1985"
' HOME RUN DERBY - ColecoVision port of the Commodore 64 game "HOME RUN
' DERBY" (BASIC + machine language), ReRUN disk Fall 1985 (also on ReRUN's
' Greatest Hits). The listing names no author.
'
' The game: a batter (left) and a pitcher (right) in a night stadium. Swing
' with the button as the pitch comes in; a hit flies to the right, and over
' the wall it is a home run (1 run). Each half inning the batting team gets
' 9 pitches (a ball - a pitch outside the zone that you let go - does not
' count). 9 innings, BLUE bats first; the most home runs wins, a tie plays on.
'
' The rules are the C64's, in its own units (sprite coordinates, steps):
' - every frame the C64 runs its delay-loop machine code for 12577 cycles
'   (measured in VICE: pitch 4.70/3.17/2.40/1.92 px a frame at speeds
'   2/3/4/5, flight 1.60 steps a frame); a step costs 106 + 1285 x speed
'   cycles while pitching and 7860 in flight (Acc, PCost)
' - the pitch ($C028): fire checked first each step, then a curve step
'   (8/256 chance, up or down, fixed per pitch), then x - 1
' - contact when the swing comes with the ball at x 28-46; it is a hit only
'   if the ball is at y 198-202, else it goes on to the catcher
' - power = 19 - 2|37 - x| - speed (the computer's speed has a random
'   fraction), at least 1; fast computer pitches add a random 1-31 to the
'   first climb (YY)
' - the flight ($C07A) is the C64's counter machine, step for step (FB, FE,
'   FD, FC, R50: doubling then halving), then 45 degrees down
' - the wall: sprite-sprite collisions latch like $D01E (Lat) and are read
'   once per flight step; only "ball + wall" starts the fall. A fall leaves
'   the latch set, so the next hit drops at once unless the pitch touched
'   the batter (the C64 does exactly that)
' - home run: the ball ends at x > 341 (past the wall)
'
' Controls: 1 PLAYER - you bat for BLUE with either controller, the
' computer bats for RED. 2 PLAYERS - controller 1 bats for BLUE, controller
' 2 for RED. PITCHES: AUTO (the computer pitches, as the C64 "N") or PLAYER
' (the fielding side pitches: hold the stick, press the button: left fast,
' right slow, up rises, down sinks, as the C64 "Y").

